1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
I think the one biggest benefit of agentic workflows is that it allows you

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:07,740
to do many tasks effectively that just previously were not possible.

3
00:00:08,240 --> 00:00:12,560
But there are other benefits as well, including parallelism that lets you do certain things

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:17,680
quite fast, as well as modularity that lets you combine the best of breed components from many

5
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:21,260
different places to build an effective workflow. Let's take a look.

6
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:28,360
My team collected some data on a coding benchmark that tests the ability of different OOMs to write

7
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:34,640
code to carry out certain tasks. The benchmark used in this case is called Human Eval, and it turns out

8
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:40,240
that GPT 3.5, this is a model that the first publicly available version of ChatGPT was based on,

9
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:45,140
if asked to write the code directly, to just type out the computer program, gets 40%

10
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:51,040
right on this benchmark. This is a positive key metric. GPT 4 is a much better model. Its performance

11
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:58,320
leaps to 67%, with this also non-agentic workflow. But it turns out that as large as the

12
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:04,400
improvement was from GPT 3.5 to GPT 4, that improvement is dwarfed by what you can achieve by

13
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:11,020
wrapping GPT 3.5 within an agentic workflow. Using different agentic techniques, which you

14
00:01:11,020 --> 00:01:16,740
learn about later in this course, you can prompt GPT 3.5 to write code and then maybe reflect on the

15
00:01:16,740 --> 00:01:22,140
code and figure out if you can improve it. And using techniques like that, you can actually get GPT 3.5

16
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:28,380
to get much higher levels of performance. And similarly GPT 4 used in the context of an agentic

17
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:35,940
workflow also does much better. So even with today's SLMs, an agentic workflow lets you get much better

18
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:41,700
performance. And in fact, what we saw in this example was the improvement from one generation of

19
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:48,000
model to another, which is huge, is still not as big a difference as implementing an agentic workflow on

20
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,580
the previous generation of model. Another benefit of using agentic workflows is that they can paralyze

21
00:01:54,580 --> 00:02:01,540
some tasks and thus do certain things much faster than a human. For example, if you ask an agentic

22
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:07,020
workflow to write an essay about black holes, you might be able to have three LMs run in parallel to

23
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:13,160
generate ideas for web search terms to type into search engine. Based on the first web search, it may

24
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:20,620
identify say three top results to fetch and based on the second web search, it may identify a second

25
00:02:20,620 --> 00:02:26,960
set of web pages to fetch and so on. And it turns out that whereas a human doing this research would

26
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:32,540
have to read these nine web pages sequentially or one at a time, when you're using an agentic workflow,

27
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:39,200
you can actually paralyze all nine web page downloads and then finally feed all these things into them

28
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:45,940
to write an essay. So even though agentic workflows do take longer than truly non-agentic workflows or by

29
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:51,720
direct generation by just prompting a single time, if you were to compare this type of agentic workflow to

30
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:57,080
how a human would have to go about the task, the ability to paralyze downloading lots of web pages

31
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:03,220
can actually let it do certain tasks much faster than the non-parallel sequential way that a single

32
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:09,080
human might process this data. To build on this example, it turns out one of the things I often do

33
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:15,760
when building agentic workflows is look at the individual components like the LM and add or swap out

34
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:21,300
components. So for example, maybe I look at the web search engine I use up here and I might decide

35
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:25,660
that I want to soften a new web search engine. When building agentic workflows, there are actually

36
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:31,340
multiple web search engines including Google, which you can access via Serpa, as well as others like

37
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:37,180
Bing, DuckDuckGo, Tavilli, u.com. There are actually quite a lot of options for web search engines

38
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:42,460
designed for LOMs to use. Or maybe instead of just doing three web searches, maybe on this step,

39
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:49,060
you can swap in a new news search engine so they can find out what's the latest news on recent

40
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:56,220
breakthroughs on black hole signs. And lastly, instead of using the same LOM for all of the different

41
00:03:56,220 --> 00:04:02,680
steps, I will often try out different large language models and maybe try out different LOM providers

42
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:07,680
to see which one gives the best result for different steps of this system.

43
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:13,320
So to summarize, the main reason I use agentic workflows is it just gives much better performance

44
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:20,440
on many different applications. But in addition, it can also paralyze some tasks that humans will

45
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:26,960
otherwise have to do sequentially. And the modular design of many agentic workflows also lets us

46
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:30,580
add or update tools and sometimes swap out models.

